# How to use this lesson

Lesson 79 defined Z=∫Dφ e^{iS}. Adding a source J(x) coupled to φ makes Z a functional of J. Differentiating with respect to J brings down fields, so time-ordered correlators become functional derivatives at J=0. For a free field the integral is Lesson 2's Gaussian and the second derivative is Lesson 45's propagator. Expect five to seven hours.

## Learning objectives

- Write Z[J]=∫Dφ exp(iS[φ]+i∫d⁴x Jφ).

- Obtain ⟨Tφ(x)φ(y)⟩=−(1/Z) δ²Z/δJ(x)δJ(y) at J=0, up to the i-counting of this course's Minkowski convention.

- Complete the square in a free theory and recover Z[J]=Z[0] exp(i/2 ∫J Δ J).

- Define W[J]=−i log Z[J] as the generator of connected correlators.

