# 1 Coupling a source

FIG:
   J(x) ---- φ(x) ---- Δ ---- φ(y) ---- J(y)     completing the square
                                                    produces a J–Δ–J line
ENDFIG

Promote Lesson 79's vacuum integral by adding i∫Jφ to the exponent:

$$ Z[J] = ∫ Dφ  exp( i S[φ] + i ∫ d⁴x J(x) φ(x) )

Each derivative δ/δJ(x) brings down a factor of iφ(x) from the exponent. Two derivatives, then J→0, produce the two-point function. With this course's iS convention,

$$ ⟨ T φ(x) φ(y) ⟩ = − (1 / Z[0])  δ²Z / δJ(x) δJ(y) |_{J=0}

the extra minus coming from two factors of i. Higher correlators follow the same pattern. This is an equivalent definition of Lesson 45's time-ordered product, not a new object.

### Worked example 1

What is δZ/δJ(x) at J=0? It is i Z[0] ⟨φ(x)⟩, which vanishes in the free theory and in QED by Lesson 30's charge conjugation (or by the oddness of the Gaussian). The first nontrivial derivative is the second.

# 2 Completing the square

For a free scalar, S[φ]=½∫φ K φ with K=−(□+m²), so

$$ S[φ] + ∫ J φ = ½ ∫ (φ + K⁻¹ J) K (φ + K⁻¹ J) − ½ ∫ J K⁻¹ J

after an integration by parts. Shifting φ (the measure Dφ is translation invariant for a linear shift) leaves

$$ Z[J] = Z[0]  exp( i/2 ∫ J Δ J )

with Δ=K⁻¹ the Feynman propagator of Lesson 45, iε included so the Gaussian converges (Lesson 66's Wick rotation). Differentiating twice recovers Δ(x−y)=⟨Tφ(x)φ(y)⟩_free.

# 3 Connected correlators

Z generates all correlators, including products of disjoint clusters (vacuum bubbles on the side). The generator of connected correlators is

$$ W[J] = − i log Z[J]

because the logarithm subtracts the disconnected pieces, exactly as log of a product of independent Gaussians becomes a sum. In the free theory, W[J]=½∫J Δ J, which is already connected: a single line joining two sources.

### Worked example 2

Why do scattering formulae use connected correlators? An extra vacuum bubble multiplies every amplitude by the same Z[0] factor and cancels in the normalized S-matrix of Lesson 50. W builds that cancellation in from the start.

# 4 QED

The same construction applies with J_μ coupled to A^μ and Grassmann sources coupled to ψ, ψ̄. Completing the square in the photon Gaussian recovers D_μν of Lesson 47, in whatever gauge Lesson 70 chose. Completing the square in the fermion Gaussian recovers S_F of Lesson 46, with a minus sign for a closed loop from the Grassmann determinant, matching Lesson 62.

